# What never belongs in a build context. All three Dockerfiles take the repository root as
# their context, so one list serves all of them: this file is about what must not be
# uploaded to the daemon at all, while each Dockerfile's COPY lines decide what reaches an
# image.

# The developer's own pixi environments - hundreds of megabytes of prefixes that the build
# materialises its own copy of, and by far the largest thing a context would otherwise
# carry. Sending them would also put a prefix built for this machine's absolute paths
# inside an image that holds it somewhere else.
.pixi/

# Git history. No build reads it, and it carries every past revision of every file.
.git/
.gitignore
.github/

# Restored inside the image by `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`.
node_modules/

# Built inside the image by vite; a stale local copy must never be able to win.
frontend/dist/

# The local PostgreSQL cluster, data directory and server log included.
backend/.pgdata/

# Bytecode and tool caches, every one of them machine-local.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.import_linter_cache/
.coverage
coverage.xml
coverage/

# Every .env variant, the two committed defaults included. They hold development values -
# a loopback database and a localhost API origin - and an image that found one would be
# configured by this checkout instead of by its deployment. The backend reads its settings
# from the environment and opens no file, so for it such a file could only mislead; the
# frontend's is inlined by vite at build time, so its image writes the value it wants
# rather than inheriting one from here. .envrc matches this rule and is excluded with
# them, nothing in any of the three images being a blessed shell.
.env*

# Editor and agent state.
.claude/
.zed/

# The deployment. It describes how the images these builds produce are run, so no build
# reads it and an image carrying it would be carrying its own deployment.
compose.yaml
